<header><p class="st-kicker">kingdom · Arabia and the Gulf</p><h1 class="st-title">Kingdom of Qataban</h1><p class="st-meta">650 BCE – 175 CE (approx.) · capital Timna</p></header>
<p class="st-teaser">An incense kingdom of south Arabia centred on Wadi Bayhan and its capital Timna, rival of Saba and Hadramawt for five centuries.</p>
<div class="st-body"><div class="st-text"><h2>Origins</h2><p>Qataban emerges in Sabaean inscriptions of the 7th century BCE as a junior ally of the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/empire/kingdom-of-saba">Kingdom of Saba</a>. As Sabaean power waned, its rulers asserted independence by the 5th or 4th century BCE, ruling from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/timna-yemen">Timna</a> (modern Hajar Kuhlan) in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/wadi-bayhan">Wadi Bayhan</a>, at the start of the caravan route north.</p><h2>Rule and society</h2><p>The kingdom taxed the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/incense">incense</a> trade and built irrigation systems in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/wadi-bayhan">Wadi Bayhan</a>. Its most famous text, a royal market law inscribed on a stele at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/timna-yemen">Timna</a>, regulated trade and taxes. Qatabanians worshipped the god Amm and wrote <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/qatabanian-language">Qatabanic</a>. At its height it reached the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gulf-of-aden">Gulf of Aden</a> coast and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/wadi-markha">Wadi Markha</a>.</p><h2>Decline and legacy</h2><p>Qataban weakened in the 1st and 2nd centuries CE as sea trade grew. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/timna-yemen">Timna</a> was burnt, and around 175 CE the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/kingdom-of-hadramawt">Kingdom of Hadramawt</a> and the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/empire/himyarite-kingdom">Himyarite Kingdom</a> divided its lands. Excavations by American teams in the 1950s made Timna one of the best-known south Arabian sites.</p></div><aside class="st-side"><div class="st-emblem" role="img" aria-label="kingdom, 650 BCE – 175 CE (approx.)"><svg viewBox="0 0 100 100" aria-hidden="true"><path d="M22 72 V40 L36 54 L50 28 L64 54 L78 40 V72 Z"/><path d="M22 80 H78"/><circle cx="50" cy="24" r="3"/><circle cx="22" cy="36" r="2.5"/><circle cx="78" cy="36" r="2.5"/></svg><p class="st-emblem-type">kingdom</p><p class="st-emblem-region">Arabia and the Gulf</p><div class="st-span" aria-hidden="true"><span class="st-span-bar" style="left:46.77%;width:16.42%"></span></div><div class="st-span-axis" aria-hidden="true"><span>3000 BCE</span><span>1 CE</span><span>2000</span></div></div><dl class="st-facts"><dt>Years</dt><dd>650 BCE – 175 CE (approx.)</dd><dt>Centuries</dt><dd><a href="/centuries/7th-century-bce">7th century BCE</a> – <a href="/centuries/2nd-century">2nd century</a></dd><dt>Type</dt><dd>kingdom</dd><dt>Character</dt><dd>mercantile</dd><dt>Capital</dt><dd>Timna</dd><dt>Rulers</dt><dd>Yada'ab Dhubyan, Shahr Yagil Yuhargib</dd><dt>Religion</dt><dd>South Arabian polytheism (Amm)</dd><dt>Language</dt><dd>Qatabanic</dd><dt>Today</dt><dd><a href="/countries/yemen">Yemen</a></dd><dt>Also</dt><dd>Qataban, Qatabanians</dd></dl><figure class="st-locator"><a href="/atlas?year=-238&amp;focus=kingdom-of-qataban" aria-label="Kingdom of Qataban on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="5932 1620 640 512" role="img" aria-label="Map: where Kingdom of Qataban lay"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-tie" d="M6252 1876L6237 1859M6252 1876L6284 1862"/><path class="map-dot-n" d="M6237 1859h0M6284 1862h0"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M6252 1876h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M6252 1876h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks Timna. <a href="/atlas?year=-238&amp;focus=kingdom-of-qataban">On the atlas &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></aside></div>

